<header>
  <h1>Progress bars</h1>
</header>

<section id="introduction">
	<p class="introduction">
		<strong>Progress bars</strong> communicate task status visually, giving users a sense of how much work remains. Use them for lengthy processes like file uploads, installations, or multi-step forms to set expectations and reduce uncertainty. Always pair progress bars with clear labels or percentages so users understand what’s happening at a glance.
	</p>
	<p class="introduction">
		<strong>Progress bars</strong> can be determinate (showing specific progress) or indeterminate (indicating ongoing activity without a set endpoint). Choose the right type based on the task: use determinate bars when you can measure progress, and indeterminate bars for processes where timing is unpredictable. For a value that is a measurement rather than a task in progress, such as disk usage or a score, use a <a href="#meters">meter</a> instead.
	</p>
	<p class="introduction">
		The native <code>&lt;progress&gt;</code> and <code>&lt;meter&gt;</code> elements are already announced correctly by screen readers, so they need no extra ARIA attributes. Give each one an accessible name with a <code>&lt;label&gt;</code>. The <code>aria-valuenow</code>, <code>aria-valuemin</code> and <code>aria-valuemax</code> attributes are only needed when you build a custom progress bar from a <code>&lt;div&gt;</code> with <code>role="progressbar"</code>.
	</p>
</section>

<section id="default">
  <header>
		<h2>Default</h2>
  </header>

	<div class="section-example">
		<progress max="100" value="2"></progress>
		<progress max="100" value="20"></progress>
		<progress max="100" value="40"></progress>
		<progress max="100" value="60"></progress>
		<progress max="100" value="80"></progress>
		<progress max="100" value="100"></progress>
	</div>

	<p>Use the <code>&lt;progress&gt;</code> element with <code>max</code> and <code>value</code> attributes to create a determinate progress bar. The <code>max</code> attribute sets the total value, while the <code>value</code> attribute indicates the current progress.</p>

  <div class="code-example">
    <pre><code class="html">&lt;progress max="..." value="..."&gt;&lt;/progress&gt;</code></pre>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="indeterminate">
  <header>
		<h2>Indeterminate</h2>
  </header>

	<p>A progress bar <strong>without</strong> a <code>value</code> attribute is indeterminate: a process is running, but how much of it is complete is unknown. The whole bar shows moving stripes instead of a filled portion. Set a <code>value</code> as soon as progress can be measured, and the bar switches to the determinate style.</p>

	<div class="section-example">
		<progress></progress>
	</div>

  <div class="code-example">
    <pre><code class="html">&lt;progress&gt;&lt;/progress&gt;</code></pre>
  </div>

	<p class="note"><strong>Reduced motion:</strong> when the visitor has asked their operating system to reduce motion, the stripes stop animating. Pair an indeterminate bar with a short text such as “Uploading…” so the state is clear without the animation.</p>

  <p class="note"><strong>E-ink:</strong> in the <a href="/documentation/foundations/theming/#eink">e-ink theme</a> the stripes stand still. An indeterminate bar then only shows that something is going on, not that it is still going on; add a text when the wait can be long.</p>
</section>

<section id="labels">
  <header>
		<h2>Labels</h2>
  </header>

	<p>Connect a <code>&lt;label&gt;</code> to the progress bar with <code>for</code> and <code>id</code>, so assistive technology can announce what the bar belongs to. The text inside the <code>&lt;progress&gt;</code> element is only shown by browsers that cannot display the bar, which makes it a good place for a plain percentage.</p>

	<div class="section-example">
		<div class="basic-form">
			<label for="progress-label-example">Uploading <em>nausikaa-and-her-maids.jpg</em> (70%)</label>
			<progress id="progress-label-example" max="100" value="70">70%</progress>
		</div>
	</div>

  <div class="code-example">
    <pre><code class="html">&lt;label for="upload-progress"&gt;Uploading photo (70%)&lt;/label&gt;
&lt;progress id="upload-progress" max="100" value="70"&gt;70%&lt;/progress&gt;</code></pre>
  </div>
</section>

<section id="colors">
  <header>
		<h2>Colors</h2>
  </header>
	<p>Customize progress bar <strong>colors</strong> using CSS classes to match your design system or brand guidelines. Below are examples of progress bars with different color classes applied.</p>

	<div class="section-example">
		<progress max="100" value="60" class="rosewater"></progress>
		<progress max="100" value="65" class="flamingo"></progress>
		<progress max="100" value="53" class="pink"></progress>
		<progress max="100" value="71" class="mauve"></progress>
		<progress max="100" value="55" class="red"></progress>
		<progress max="100" value="76" class="maroon"></progress>
		<progress max="100" value="67" class="peach"></progress>
		<progress max="100" value="45" class="yellow"></progress>
		<progress max="100" value="83" class="green"></progress>
		<progress max="100" value="71" class="teal"></progress>
		<progress max="100" value="90" class="sky"></progress>
		<progress max="100" value="55" class="sapphire"></progress>
		<progress max="100" value="73" class="blue"></progress>
		<progress max="100" value="53" class="lavender"></progress>
		<progress max="100" value="71" class="gray"></progress>
	</div>

	<p class="note"><strong>Catppuccin hues</strong> are available for progress bars as well; reference the <a href="https://catppuccin.com" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Catppuccin palette</a> to keep status treatments consistent with the rest of the system.</p>

  <div class="code-example">
    <pre><code class="html">&lt;progress class="rosewater"&gt;&lt;/progress&gt;
&lt;progress class="flamingo"&gt;&lt;/progress&gt;
&lt;progress class="pink"&gt;&lt;/progress&gt;
&lt;progress class="mauve"&gt;&lt;/progress&gt;
&lt;progress class="red"&gt;&lt;/progress&gt;
&lt;progress class="maroon"&gt;&lt;/progress&gt;
&lt;progress class="peach"&gt;&lt;/progress&gt;
&lt;progress class="yellow"&gt;&lt;/progress&gt;
&lt;progress class="green"&gt;&lt;/progress&gt;
&lt;progress class="teal"&gt;&lt;/progress&gt;
&lt;progress class="sky"&gt;&lt;/progress&gt;
&lt;progress class="sapphire"&gt;&lt;/progress&gt;
&lt;progress class="blue"&gt;&lt;/progress&gt;
&lt;progress class="lavender"&gt;&lt;/progress&gt;
&lt;progress class="gray"&gt;&lt;/progress&gt;</code></pre>

  </div>

  <p class="note"><strong>E-ink:</strong> in the <a href="/documentation/foundations/theming/#eink">e-ink theme</a> every progress bar is black, whatever its color class.</p>
</section>

<section id="meters">
  <header>
		<h2>Meters</h2>
  </header>

	<p>A <strong>meter</strong> shows a measurement within a known range, such as storage used, a password’s strength or a rating. Unlike a progress bar it doesn’t describe a task that will finish. Set <code>min</code>, <code>max</code> and <code>value</code>, then use <code>low</code>, <code>high</code> and <code>optimum</code> to tell the browser which part of the range is good. The bar turns green, yellow or red depending on where the value falls.</p>

	<div class="section-example">
		<div class="basic-form">
			<label for="meter-example-1">25 of 100 GB used</label>
			<meter id="meter-example-1" min="0" max="100" low="70" high="90" optimum="0" value="25">25 GB</meter>

			<label for="meter-example-2">80 of 100 GB used</label>
			<meter id="meter-example-2" min="0" max="100" low="70" high="90" optimum="0" value="80">80 GB</meter>

			<label for="meter-example-3">96 of 100 GB used</label>
			<meter id="meter-example-3" min="0" max="100" low="70" high="90" optimum="0" value="96">96 GB</meter>
		</div>
	</div>

  <div class="code-example">
    <pre><code class="html">&lt;label for="storage"&gt;Storage used: 80 of 100 GB&lt;/label&gt;
&lt;meter id="storage" min="0" max="100" low="70" high="90" optimum="0" value="80"&gt;80 GB&lt;/meter&gt;</code></pre>
  </div>

	<table class="striped">
		<thead>
			<tr><th>Attribute</th><th>Meaning</th></tr>
		</thead>
		<tbody>
			<tr><th><code>min</code>, <code>max</code></th><td>The bounds of the range. They default to 0 and 1.</td></tr>
			<tr><th><code>value</code></th><td>The current measurement.</td></tr>
			<tr><th><code>low</code>, <code>high</code></th><td>Split the range into a low, middle and high part.</td></tr>
			<tr><th><code>optimum</code></th><td>The preferred value. The part of the range that contains it is shown green, the part next to it yellow and the part furthest away red.</td></tr>
		</tbody>
	</table>

	<p>In the example above <code>optimum="0"</code> means that a low value is best. For a value where more is better, such as a battery level, set <code>optimum</code> to the maximum instead.</p>
</section>

<section id="demo">
  <header>
		<h2>Demo</h2>
  </header>
	<p>Click the button to see the progress bar fill up from 0 to 100%.</p>

	<div class="section-example">
		<div style="display: flex; gap: 1rem; align-items: center;">
			<progress style="flex-grow: 99" max="100" value="0" id="progressBarExample" aria-label="Demo progress"></progress>
			<button type="button" id="progressButtonExample">Progress!</button>
		</div>
	</div>

  <div class="code-example">
    <pre><code class="javascript">const progressBar = document.getElementById("progress-bar");
let value = 0;

const timer = setInterval(() =&gt; {
  value = Math.min(value + 1, 100);
  progressBar.value = value;
  if (value === 100) clearInterval(timer);
}, 20);</code></pre>
  </div>

<script>
(function () {
  var progressBar = document.getElementById("progressBarExample");
  var progressButton = document.getElementById("progressButtonExample");
  var timer = null;

  progressButton.addEventListener("click", function () {
    var value = 0;
    clearInterval(timer);
    progressBar.value = value;
    timer = setInterval(function () {
      value = Math.min(value + 1, 100);
      progressBar.value = value;
      if (value === 100) clearInterval(timer);
    }, 20);
  });
})();
</script>

</section>
